HERO_STR_STATIC(hero_str_177e8c0b, "` are both ");
HERO_STR_STATIC(hero_str_49a9f243, " over `");
HERO_STR_STATIC(hero_str_4bcb60d7, "` \342\200\224 one tag, one of each kind");
HERO_STR_STATIC(hero_str_71ca9f56, "two Heroes types over ONE C type is a hole nothing can close: this compiler refuses handing one where the other belongs, and clang accepts it, so the mistake survives every check the language has. Give the two types two tags, or give the one type one name and use it twice");
HERO_STR_STATIC(hero_str_6e222984, "one tag may carry a HANDLE and a record WITH FIELDS, because those are two different C types \342\200\224 `struct ");
HERO_STR_STATIC(hero_str_342f3ed8, " *` and `struct ");
HERO_STR_STATIC(hero_str_2bffd9a0, "` \342\200\224 and clang refuses a swap between them on its own. That pair is how a struct C hands back by pointer is both pointed at and read (\302\247 13). What is refused here is two of the SAME kind");
HERO_STR_STATIC(hero_str_1795a2a8, "it is a handle, an address C owns");
HERO_STR_STATIC(hero_str_29a76b, "` is a handle, an address C owns");
HERO_STR_STATIC(hero_str_35c103b1, "handle_map_key");
HERO_STR_STATIC(hero_str_6050c8cd, "` cannot be a map key, because ");
HERO_STR_STATIC(hero_str_5a553140, "two handles are equal when their addresses are, and C recycles an address: close one and the next allocation can hand back the same bytes, so a value stored under the closed handle is found again by a handle that has nothing to do with it. Key the map on something the program owns \342\200\224 an `i64` the library gives you, or a `str` \342\200\224 and keep the handle in the value");
HERO_STR_STATIC(hero_str_9e6d981, "no library");
